From a7c895fdb89be06a6b03f9d528dbd622ecf51064 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Atomsk <135618857+Atomsk-debug@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 14:20:48 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 5/6] Virtual amiibo: with DM_AMIIBO naming an amiibo ID, the
 NFC device finds that amiibo, and the game reads its identity and keeps its
 application area

The host has no NFC reader, and every nfp tag call answered "tag not found", so no game's amiibo feature could be used. amiibo.h is a virtual tag: DM_AMIIBO is an
amiibo ID (16 hex digits) or a file holding one, read again at each StartDetection so the amiibo can be swapped or taken away between scans. Detection finds it and
signals the activate event; GetTagInfo gives a 7-byte NTAG215 UID made from the ID, GetModelInfo the ID, GetCommonInfo the write counter and the date,
GetRegisterInfo the host's default Mii as owner (hle_mii.c dm_mii_default_charinfo) and DM_AMIIBO_NAME as nickname. The application area is created (written at
once), opened by access ID, read, set and kept by Flush, and an unflushed area is dropped at Unmount and StopDetection; it is stored as <ID>.area in
DM_AMIIBO_DIR, else in an amiibo folder beside DM_SAVE, made only when an amiibo is named. Device states and results follow the nfp service (Ryujinx's and yuzu's
tables). With an amiibo named, nfc::IsNfcEnabled answers true, matching nfp. No dump or key is read. Without DM_AMIIBO every answer is as before.

tests/t_amiibo.c checks the structures, every state and result, the stored area across scans, a second game's access ID, a damaged file and a missing folder.
t_nfc.c runs a whole scan through the registered hooks, with its earlier checks unchanged; built off the Mac against a stub of HLE_FORCE, its 95 checks pass.
Not run with a game: the extended application area and the amiibo settings applets still answer no tag.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
(cherry picked from commit 3101348b2be76ec309170cd6d4a1d93f64c8da41)
