+- **Fix:** `DM_AMIIBO` names an amiibo by its ID (16 hex digits, as amiibo databases list them), or a file holding one, read again at every `StartDetection`, so writing another ID swaps the amiibo between scans and emptying the file takes it away. `StartDetection` then finds it and signals the activate event; `GetTagInfo` gives a 7-byte NTAG215 UID made from the ID (the same tag at every scan), `GetModelInfo` the ID's first seven bytes, `GetCommonInfo` the write counter and today's date, `GetRegisterInfo` the host's default Mii as owner and `DM_AMIIBO_NAME` (else `amiibo`) as nickname. The application area is created (written at once), opened by its access ID, read, set and kept by `Flush`, as Horizon does; `Unmount` and `StopDetection` drop a set area that was not flushed. It is stored as `<ID>.area` in `DM_AMIIBO_DIR`, else in an `amiibo` folder beside `DM_SAVE`, made only when an amiibo is named. No amiibo dump or key is read: a game sees the figure's identity and the data it writes itself, not data a Switch wrote to a real figure. Device states and results follow the `nfp` service (Ryujinx's and yuzu's tables): 2 found, 4 mounted; 73 wrong state, 128 no area, 152 another game's area, 168 an area already there, 68 an area too large. `amiibo.h` holds it; without `DM_AMIIBO` every answer is as before. `dm/host/tests/t_amiibo.c` checks the structures, states, results and the stored area; `t_nfc.c` runs a whole scan through the hooks.
